WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.120
Good Morning John.

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:08.860
I want to start this off by saying that I
am not a doctor, I'm not a counselor, I haven't

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:13.629
experienced sexual abuse, I'm just a person
with some thoughts that hopefully could be

00:00:13.629 --> 00:00:14.629
helpful.

00:00:14.629 --> 00:00:15.629
To someone.

00:00:15.629 --> 00:00:18.680
Sex is complicated, in like, every way imaginable.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:23.300
This is not aided by the fact that we have
a general societal taboo against discussing

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:24.300
it.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:27.400
So when we do talk about it, it's often shrouded
in metaphors and innuendo and bombasticity.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:32.750
That means a lot of the sexual norms of our
culture are mysterious, and we don't actually

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:34.239
talk about them; we just sort of infer them.

00:00:34.239 --> 00:00:35.800
I think that's dumb.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:38.820
And that's part of why I helped Nick and Lindsey
start sexplanations.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:42.149
I think that our culture has kind of a messed
up relationship with sex.

00:00:42.149 --> 00:00:48.230
We somehow have made these relationships,
uh, into, like, predator/prey relationships.

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:54.980
Like, one person is the, you know, the cruise
missile of desire, and the other one is the

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:56.519
sweet, chaste little kitten.

00:00:56.519 --> 00:01:00.250
And the cruise missile has to get the kitten
and the kitten runs away.

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:02.159
I don't know why it's a cruise missile and
a kitten.

00:01:02.159 --> 00:01:04.260
I do know, though, that this is a dumb system.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:10.760
I think that a lot of the joy and wonder and
excitement of a romantic relationship comes

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:16.580
from those moments of excitedly discovering
that both parties are into this.

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:18.760
We're in- we're both into this!

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:19.760
That's a great feeling!

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:23.860
The attack missile/kitten relationship does
not encourage that.

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:27.170
I wanna be clear, I'm explaining culture but
I'm not excusing the behavior.

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:31.210
When we're set up to assume that the kitten
is gonna run whether the kitten wants the

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:34.370
cruise missile or not, that enables abuse.

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:39.720
In my opinion this is, I'm sure not all of
the reason, but one of of the big reasons

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:43.330
why sexual abuse is so common in our culture.

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:46.690
Okay, I said sexual abuse, but what- what
is sexual abuse?

00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:50.250
Legally, the definition has to be very specific,
so that it can stand up in court.

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:52.510
So it's different state to state, country
to country.

00:01:52.510 --> 00:01:57.530
But for the purposes of having a general definition,
let's just say that sexual abuse is when one

00:01:57.530 --> 00:02:02.090
party is being coerced into doing something
sexual that they do not want to do.

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:06.170
That can be because the victim is incapacitated,
or because the abuser is an authority figure,

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:09.060
or because the victim is placed in a dangerous
situation where they feel like they don't

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:12.230
have the option of saying no or getting out
of the situation.

00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:15.840
The gender of the parties is irrelevant, it
can be sex, it can be kissing.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:18.920
If one person doesn't want to be doing what
they are doing and they are being coerced

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:21.170
or pressured into doing it, then that is abuse.

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:23.200
And it is ubiquitous.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:26.720
And it needs to not be, because it holds us
back as a culture.

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:32.819
We have to rid ourselves of that outdated
and rotten conception that sexual relationships

00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:34.239
are like predator/prey things.

00:02:34.239 --> 00:02:35.840
Let's zoom in for this.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:41.180
Romantic relationships can be wonderful, but
you have to have that magical thing, consent.

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:46.430
And not saying no is not the same as saying
yes.

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:48.849
We need to communicate, we need to be sensitive,
we need to talk about what we do and don't

00:02:48.849 --> 00:02:53.849
want to do, and we have to respect and NOT
pressure people when they don't want to do

00:02:53.849 --> 00:02:54.849
something.

00:02:54.849 --> 00:02:58.230
This video is part of a long conversation
that is going to continue to be had for a

00:02:58.230 --> 00:02:59.230
long time.

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:01.239
But John and I have some ideas about how to
keep it moving.

00:03:01.239 --> 00:03:04.579
First, we've already started working with
a group of nerdfighters, including some survivors

00:03:04.579 --> 00:03:09.640
of sexual abuse, who will be working as a
task force against abuse and assault.

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:14.069
Second, we want to produce and fund a series
of videos that discusses abuse and consent

00:03:14.069 --> 00:03:18.810
and sexual relationships, especially in light
of the new digital world, like where does

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:20.499
Skype fit into it all, right?

00:03:20.499 --> 00:03:24.599
And third, we're looking to partner with some
existing sexual assault organizations to bring

00:03:24.599 --> 00:03:29.890
their work and their resources into our community,
and hopefully into other online communities

00:03:29.890 --> 00:03:30.890
as well.

00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:32.889
John, I know we're not gonna fix the whole
world but I'm pleased to be in a position

00:03:32.889 --> 00:03:37.389
where we can at least start to help enable
some positive change.

00:03:37.389 --> 00:03:38.829
I'll see you on Tuesday.

00:03:38.829 --> 00:03:42.159
If you're in an abusive relationship or you
think you might be and you're just not sure,

00:03:42.159 --> 00:03:46.060
there are links in the description to help
you figure out where you're at, and to people

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:48.239
who you can call or go to for help.

00:03:48.239 --> 00:03:52.099
Sometimes just figuring out the abusive nature
of a relationship can be a huge challenge,

00:03:52.099 --> 00:03:57.209
and once you're in there, it can seem like
it is impossible to escape, but it is not.

